WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:03.519
. Let's go next to our caller from the eight
one six area code.

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:06.000
Who's calling today from eight one six.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.719
Hello, this is David from Zara.

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:10.719
Hey David.

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:11.719
What's going on?

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:12.719
Alright.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:17.550
Um, I want to know what your opinion is.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:27.000
It seems to me, I've always been of the opinion
that one of the main reasons that we have

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.470
Trump in the white house today is because
of the lack of the democratic party ability

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.660
to work together and consolidate their power.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:44.780
The thing that you know, because I think it's
pretty obvious that had Hillary and Bernie

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.240
ran together in 2016 Trump would not be in
the white house.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:56.320
And I don't see anyone discussing for 2020
but whenever we go through these primaries,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:04.030
whoever is the top dog needs to pick the number
two as his running mate no matter what.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.230
Because that way you cast the widest net and
get the best chance of getting Trump out of

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.230
there.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.230
I mean,

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.230
well let's explore that David

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.290
80 and see what happens.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.900
I think you're correct.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:22.440
That had, so the question is by choosing Bernie
instead of Tim Cain in 2016 would Hillary

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.230
Clinton have gained more votes than she lost?

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.060
And I think the answer is yes.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.150
And I think that number would have been more
than 75,000 in those three key States.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.150
So I agree with you.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.700
I think if Hillary went with, listen, there
were so many, so many individual differences

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.701
that would have so many factors that could
have been the difference maker in 2016 but

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:47.440
I think if you have Hillary, Bernie instead
of Hillary, Tim Kaine, I think Hillary wins.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.760
Now, I do think that it's not always as simple
as the number one should pick the number two

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:02.229
because there are cases where the number one
and number twos contingents may not have two

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:06.060
and one respectively as the second choice.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.390
So I do think that it requires looking in
a little bit more detailed than simply saying

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.970
the front runner should pick the second place
person.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.040
But I do think in most cases, you're probably
right about that.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.440
Now here's another question for you.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:23.940
Do you think that it would also have a better
chance of casting the widest net if you have

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.290
a progressive and a centrist run together
in 2020

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.060
well, that is where you throw a monkey wrench
into this idea of going with number one and

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:38.700
number two, a priori, regardless of whether
they are both part of the establishment contingent

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.700
or not.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:46.290
Uh, I happen to think that in this particular
election, uh, running to the middle is a mistake

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:52.840
no matter what I would go so far as to say
to Progressive's would be better than a progressive

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:58.560
and a centrist because I don't think the centrists
are going to vote for, uh, the, the Centrus

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.620
left Democrats are going to go and vote for
Donald Trump.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.620
I think.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.620
Sure.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.849
There are some people who are kind of have
the brake, tear it all down to build it back

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:08.570
up mentality who are on the left who might
vote for Trump.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.600
And some of them did in 2016 we saw.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:18.200
But my instinct is that you were not going
to see a Bandon moment from the sort of Biden

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.550
democratic wing if you have something like
a Bernie Warren ticket.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.120
So I think that this election is unique in
that way.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.120
Yeah.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.220
Well there, there also, I have no, but you
know, it has been discussed many times about

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:36.370
how there are, there were quite a few Obama
voters that ended up voting for Trump and

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.710
now there is a certain percentage out there
of people.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:44.190
I mean, I think a lot of these people who
are, these people would have lost at life

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:49.410
in some way and are just desperate for change
in whatever form it takes.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.580
Throw anything against the wall that's different
and see if it sticks

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.580
that exists.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.890
And in fact, CNN interviewed a group of women,
some of whom claimed to have voted Obama in

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.319
2012 and then Trump in 2016 and I'll, I'll
be honest, those they didn't seem particularly

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:06.830
bright and they weren't making a lot of sense.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:10.129
So I think you're right that sometimes just
the idea generally of change is appealing

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:11.129
to people.

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:12.129
A good, it's insights.

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:13.129
David.

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:14.129
I appreciate the call.

00:04:14.129 --> 00:04:15.129
Thanks.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:16.129
All right, take care.

00:04:16.129 --> 00:04:18.139
Great to hear from uh, David in Missouri.

